KW  - Vietnamese summarization
KW  - reinforcement learning with verifier guidance
KW  - reward shaping
KW  - penalty shaping
KW  - small language models
KW  - PPO
KW  - GRPO
AB  - Reinforcement learning (RL) can optimize abstractive summarization toward task quality criteria, but imperfect rewards can cause reward hacking, factual drift, and unstable updates. This issue is acute for Vietnamese summarization, where references of high quality and human preference data at scale are costly, while deployment often favors small language models. We propose an RL framework with verifier guidance and penalty shaping. The framework uses the frozen MultiEvalSumViet2 evaluator to score source document and summary pairs for faithfulness, coherence, and relevance, then converts these scores into bounded rewards. We compare Verified Reward (VR), which directly optimizes the evaluator reward, with Verified Reward with Penalty (VRP), which adds a hard gate for clear failures and calibrated soft penalties for borderline outputs. We evaluate Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) on ViT5-large, Llama-3.2-1B, and Qwen3-0.6B under supervised fine tuning (SFT) and without SFT (noSFT) initialization. Vietnamese news experiments show improved rubric quality, with strong gains for ViT5 after SFT and Qwen3 under noSFT, while Llama benefits more clearly from GRPO than PPO. Component ablations, 95% confidence intervals, and human review reliability analysis clarify the contribution of VRP. A 625-example WikiLingua test suggests transfer beyond the news domain. Overall, the framework improves small Vietnamese summarization models while reducing reliance on reference summaries.
