TI  - Value alignment in sustainable digital marketing communication: a conceptual framework integrating strategic communication and prosocial values
T2  - FRONTIERS IN COMMUNICATION
LA  - English
KW  - digital marketing communication
KW  - digital sustainability
KW  - greenwashing
KW  - prosocial values
KW  - stakeholder engagement
KW  - strategic communication
KW  - sustainability communication
KW  - sustainable marketing
KW  - CORPORATE SOCIAL-RESPONSIBILITY
KW  - CSR COMMUNICATION
KW  - ENGAGEMENT
KW  - EMERGENCE
AB  - This conceptual article examines sustainable digital marketing communication as a process of value alignment across organizational and societal contexts. Building on strategic communication, prosocial values, CSR communication, sustainability communication, and greenwashing research, it argues that sustainability-oriented communication in digital environments should not be reduced to promotional messaging or technological adoption. Instead, it requires coordination among material practices, cultural values, stakeholder expectations, institutional supports, and ethical responsibilities. The article develops a 2 & times; 2 framework that distinguishes organizational and societal levels as well as material and cultural dimensions of sustainability. It then extends this structure through an interaction matrix, a value-alignment model, and selected interaction mechanisms to explain how sustainability claims are coordinated, interpreted, and evaluated. The framework clarifies how credible sustainable digital marketing communication depends on alignment among organizational practices, organizational values, societal infrastructures, and public expectations. It also conceptualizes greenwashing as a form of value misalignment that may emerge when sustainability claims are selective, symbolic, weakly supported, or disconnected from stakeholder expectations. By proposing research propositions for future empirical testing, the article contributes to sustainable marketing and sustainability communication research by offering a conceptual basis for examining credibility, trust, legitimacy, stakeholder engagement, and greenwashing skepticism in digital environments.
