KW  - AI governance
AB  - Dominant publishing workflows took shape under conditions of relative epistemic stability: slower problem cycles, bounded corpora, and institutions better able to absorb delay. Those conditions now hold unevenly, and in many fast-moving fields they no longer match the tempo of the object itself. Claims can become decision-relevant within days or weeks, while formal validation still unfolds over months. The result is a widening gap between what begins to circulate with authority and what has actually been epistemically stabilised. This article treats generative AI not as the origin of that problem, nor as a substitute for scholarly judgement, but as an intensifier of pressures already building within contemporary systems of validation. Using conceptual-design research, it identifies recurring breakdown patterns in scholarly publishing and translates them into explicit design requirements. From there, it proposes Living Knowledge Infrastructure (LKI) as a redesign of scholarly validation for an era in which generative AI accelerates knowledge production faster than existing publishing systems can verify it. It advances a sociotechnical framework for sustaining cumulative verification beyond the version of record. The contribution is architectural and testable: LKI links pedagogical review, executable knowledge, journals as knowledge stewards, and epistemic care into a model designed to preserve review continuity, lower verification costs, and strengthen corrigibility under accelerated conditions. Rather than calling simply for faster or more open dissemination, the article argues for a linked redesign of how scholarly judgement is recorded, carried across versions, and made publicly accountable when uptake begins to outrun validation.
