TI  - Why Go Heavy? Rethinking Visual Information Reduction in EEG-to-Image Alignment
T2  - 2026 14TH INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON BRAIN-COMPUTER INTERFACE, BCI
LA  - English
CP  - 14th International Conference on Brain Computer Interface-BCI-Annual
KW  - Brain-AI Alignment
KW  - Brain Decoding
KW  - Brain-Computer Interface
KW  - EEG
AB  - EEG-based visual brain decoding has recently gained increasing attention, as it seeks to align brain signals with visual representations to infer aspects of human visual perception. Recent approaches typically leverage pre-trained vision encoders (e.g., CLIP and DINO) for EEG-image alignment, and several studies have shown that applying image augmentations can further improve alignment. However, despite this progress, there is still no systematic analysis clarifying which visual encoders, or augmentation strategies most effectively facilitate EEG-image alignment. In this work, we present a comprehensive benchmark evaluating EEG-to-image alignment across thirteen pre-trained visual encoders and four EEG encoders, along with five augmentation strategies. Our findings s how that (i) CNN-based visual backbones consistently outperform ViT-based models, (ii) smaller visual models yield stronger alignment, revealing a negative correlation between model scale and alignment quality; and (iii) augmentations that reduce visual detail substantially improve alignment across all encoders. These results provide practical guidelines for selecting and designing models for EEG-vision alignment and highlight the importance of controlling visual information complexity in cross-modal representation learning.
