KW  - PRIVACY PARADOX
KW  - BIG DATA
KW  - FARMERS
KW  - INNOVATION
KW  - ADOPTION
AB  - Artificial intelligence-based decision support systems (AI-DSS) are reshaping agricultural decision making by fusing real time and historical data to improve productivity, environmental performance, and climate resilience. Beyond prediction, contemporary tools deliver classification, prescriptions, and scheduling, linking ecosystem services to on-farm management, markets, and policy. Yet adoption in agriculture is uneven, often reflecting misalignment between system design and user needs. We quantify these trade-offs using a discrete choice experiment with Certified Crop Advisors (CCAs) across North America and estimate preferences with a Bayesian hierarchical multinomial logit. The experiment varies four core features; cost, predictive accuracy, spatial precision, and data ownership, to reveal how advisors value configurations under realistic market bundles and an opt-out status quo. Results show that technical performance matters, but cost and ownership, especially shared or open models, are pivotal to selection. CCAs also favor systems that augment rather than replace professional judgment, valuing editable workflows, local calibration, and field verification. These patterns motivate an AI alignment approach to agricultural AI-DSS that pairs model performance with data governance, transparency, and human in the loop design. We conclude that developers and policymakers should co-create with users so that tools are trusted, equitable, and context sensitive, and so that benefits scale across diverse farms and advisory contexts.
