AB  - The rise of the gig economy has reshaped workforce structures and challenged traditional Human Resource Management (HRM) models, making it crucial to understand managers' perspectives on integrating freelancers - an area still underexplored, especially in emerging economies. This study addresses this gap by examining Romanian managers' perceptions of hiring freelancers, using unique survey data. Drawing on the theoretical foundations of Person-Organization Fit theory and Human Capital theory, following rigorous econometrical analysis, this research shows that managers' desire to engage freelancers is influenced not only by technical expertise but also by cultural fit, value alignment, knowledge, and willingness to share knowledge within organizational teams. Furthermore, demographic (age, gender) and industry-level differences significantly shape managerial attitudes. These insights contribute to HRM scholarship by advancing theory on managerial decision-making in relation to non-standard work arrangements, with direct practical implications.
