AU  - Hutcherson, N
TI  - A Model of Engagement in Hostile Legislative Environments: University General Counsel's Approaches to Advising Institutional Leaders
T2  - JOURNAL OF HIGHER EDUCATION
LA  - English
KW  - Educational attorneys
KW  - higher education leaders
KW  - institutional values
KW  - equity
KW  - policymakers
KW  - race-conscious policy
KW  - engagement
KW  - COLLEGE
AB  - As higher education institutions and their leaders face increased scrutiny and legislative attack, university general counsel (UGC), the lead attorneys for colleges and universities, play a critical role in navigating and countering these challenges. Policymakers' attack on colleges and universities only increased after the Supreme Court's race-conscious admissions decision. Current research does not reveal how UGC might navigate a hostile legislative environment when advising institutional leaders or whether they promote educational values like racial equity through their advising. Based on a case study of UGC's influence on institutional leadership, this particular study describes the unique and powerful influence UGC have on institutional leadership in response to legislative challenges. Offering a preliminary model of engagement, these approaches include: a) anticipating legislative intrusiveness through legislative monitoring and scenario planning, b) evaluating tensions between legislation and institutional policy, such as policy audits and assessments of institutional risk tolerance, c) assessing the sociopolitical environment to support institutional leaders' policy development by conducting stakeholder analysis, and d) considering institutional value alignment as they advise institutional leaders about institutional responses. The approaches UGC took in the midst of legislation targeting equity initiatives in higher education have implications for theory, research, policy, and practice.
