KW  - ADJUSTMENT
KW  - IMPACT
AB  - Purpose- This study aims to redirect the sustainable development conversation from a largely macro-level focus to a micro-level, individual-centred viewpoint within organisational contexts. It also aims to conceptualise the integration of sustainable practices at the employee level by introducing the concept of a "green citizen". Design/methodology/approach- Drawing from the literature on newcomer socialisation, person-organisation fit and organisational identification, the paper formulates propositions and suggests an initial model for nurturing "green citizens" within organisations. Findings- It is suggested that sustainable development in organisations can be effectively promoted through sustainable hiring practices and specific socialisation processes that enhance strong organisational identification and value alignment. Research limitations/implications- By emphasising the role of individual organisational members in promoting sustainability, this study opens new pathways for micro-level research on sustainable development. This encourages researchers to investigate how organisational practices and employee experiences interact to influence sustainable outcomes at the employee level. Originality/value- The authors offer a novel theoretical contribution by linking macro-level sustainability standards with micro-level organisational behaviour. This opens a new perspective on embedding sustainability into the organisational framework, moving beyond policy and structure to emphasise individual agency and identity.
