TI  - Real-time prices and sustainability metrics in retail: Impacts on satisfaction, value, and repurchase intent
T2  - JOURNAL OF RETAILING AND CONSUMER SERVICES
LA  - English
KW  - Digital shelf labels
KW  - Dynamic pricing
KW  - Eco-score
KW  - Customer satisfaction
KW  - Perceived value
KW  - Repurchase intent
KW  - Omnichannel grocery retail
KW  - Field experiment
AB  - Customer satisfaction in grocery retail is increasingly shaped by digital touchpoints, yet existing research has largely examined traditional drivers such as product quality, price level, and convenience in isolation and under controlled laboratory conditions. How emerging digital shelf cues, particularly real-time price updates delivered through electronic shelf labels and product-level sustainability scores, jointly influence post-purchase satisfaction, perceived value, and repurchase intent across product categories and shopping channels remains poorly understood. This gap is consequential because omnichannel retailers worldwide are rapidly deploying these technologies without empirical guidance on their combined consumer-experience effects. To address this gap, we conducted a 12-week cluster-randomised field experiment across 48 omnichannel supermarkets in Canada, Spain, and China. Three high-volume product categories, breakfast cereals, bottled beverages, and chilled ready meals, were each exposed to one of four digital shelf treatments: (1) static price with no eco-score (control), (2) dynamic price only, (3) eco-score only, and (4) dynamic price combined with eco-score. Transaction-linked survey data from 7412 shoppers, collected through two-wave SMS instruments and merged with point-of-sale records and mobile-app telemetry, were analysed using hierarchical Bayesian multilevel models. Results show that dynamic pricing, when framed as transparent smart savings, meaningfully improved overall satisfaction by strengthening perceived price fairness. Eco-scores independently enhanced satisfaction through perceived quality signalling and ethical value alignment, with the strongest effects observed in chilled ready meals, where sensory uncertainty is highest. The combined treatment produced an additive satisfaction gain exceeding either cue alone, without eroding category-level margins. Mediation analyses confirmed that price fairness and moral congruence operate as parallel psychological mechanisms, while moderation tests showed that high-frequency online shoppers respond more strongly to dynamic pricing, whereas in-store buyers are more receptive to ecoscores. The findings apply to affluent, digitally literate markets and to downward, savings-framed price adjustments; their boundary conditions for upward price moves, price-sensitive emerging economies, technologyverified provenance signals, layered-information cognitive load, algorithmic personalization ethics, and longterm trust dynamics are mapped as priority directions for future research. This study makes three principal contributions. First, it provides the first large-scale, multi-country field-experimental evidence on how digitally delivered economic and ethical shelf cues independently and jointly shape customer satisfaction in live grocery environments. Second, it identifies the mediating roles of transparency-driven fairness and sustainability-driven moral congruence, extending price-fairness and value-congruence theories into the digital shelf era. Third, it shows meaningful category and channel heterogeneity, offering retailers actionable guidance for calibrating realtime pricing engines alongside credible sustainability metrics to enhance customer experience without compromising profitability.
