AB  - Objective Infrared spectroscopy integrated with chemometrics is pivotal for sample detection across diverse fields. However, its accuracy is compromised by high dimensionality, spectral peak overlap, and cross-sensitivity, particularly in low-volume fraction multi-component gas mixtures like methane, ethane, propane, and butane isomers encountered in gas logging during oil exploration. Traditional machine learning methods and neural networks require laborious preprocessing and lack robustness against severe nonlinearities. This study addresses these limitations by proposing a hybrid deep learning model called Spectral Transformer, to enhance quantitative analysis precision for seven alkane gases, enabling reliable real-time reservoir assessment in challenging logging environments. Methods A dynamic gas-mixing system is used to configure volume fractions (0-1 % ) of seven alkanes using Alicat Scientific flowmeters (+/- 0.01% full scale accuracy). Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (Bruker INVENIO, SNR 60000, resolution 0.4 cm(-1)) is used to generate 3537 samples (1866 absorbance points each) across 400-4000 cm(-1) (Figs. 1 and 2). We propose the Spectral Transformer, a novel architecture that synergistically integrates convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and Transformer mechanisms. The model consists of two core components: Conv Blocks and Transformer Block. The Conv Blocks are responsible for extracting local spectral features through residual-connected convolutional layers with kernel sizes of 1 and 3, combines with multilayer perceptron (MLPs). The Transformer Block captures global dependency by multi-head self-attention (MHSA) (Eqs. 1 and 2), where input features are transformed into outputs via successive MHSA and MLP operations (Eqs. 3 and 4). Through hybrid stacking, optimal performance is achieved using a configuration of two Conv Blocks followed by one Transformer Block (Fig. 3). Hyperparameters (batch size, learning rate, kernel size, attention heads) are tuned using Gaussian process regression and expected improvement (EI) acquisition (Eqs. 5 and 6), minimizing prediction root mean square error (RMSE). Performances are assessed via RMSE of calibration (RMSEC), RMSE of prediction (RMSEP), coefficient of determination in calibration (R-C(2)), and coefficient of determination in prediction (R-P(2)) (Eqs. 7 and 8). Models are trained (2829 samples) and tested (708 samples) using PyTorch on an NVIDIA RTX 2080 Ti workstation. Results and Discussions Bayesian optimization reduces RMSEP by 13.0 % -47.4 % across all gases (Table 4). For example, methane RMSEP decreases from 8.97x10(-6) to 4.89x 10(-6). Different orders have different effects on predicted performance, among three stacking orders, Conv Block+Conv Block+Transformer Block stacking yields lowest RMSEP (4.89x10(-6) for methane), while the RMSEP of methane for Transformer Block+Conv Block+Conv Block is 9.62x10(-6) (Table 5). As the number of Block stacks changes, the prediction results also vary to some extent. When three groups of Blocks are stacked, the RMSEP reaches the optimal value of 4.89x 10(-6), which is lower than the RMSEP of the two-group stacking (7.52x 10(-6)) and the five-group stacking (8.12x 10(-6)) (Table 6). The proposed model yields different prediction values for different gases. The prediction result for n-butane gas is the worst, with an RMSEP of 8.
   63x 10(-6), while the prediction result for isobutane gas is the best, with an RMSEP of 2.60x10(-6) (Table 7). The RMSEP for all gases is less than 1x10(-5), and the coefficient of determination is 0.99 for all of them. Scatter plots confirms strong prediction-real value alignment (Fig. 4). The proposed Spectral Transformer model demonstrates superior performance, surpassing established benchmarks in multi-component gas analysis via infrared spectroscopy. The model effectively addresses key limitations of previous approaches: conventional CNN, constrained by limited long-range feature capture, achieves a mean RMSEP of 3.114x 10(-5); long short-term memory networks (LSTM), which is inefficient for processing long sequences, yields a mean RMSEP of 2.699x 10(-5); and the CNN meet vision Transformers (CMT) model results in a mean RMSEP of 1.107x10(-5) (Fig. 5 and Table 8). In contrast, the novel hybrid architecture of the Spectral Transformer reduces the mean RMSEP to 5.20x10(-6), marking an 80% improvement over traditional CNN. Conclusions The Spectral Transformer model effectively addresses critical challenges in infrared spectroscopy for quantitative analysis. Its success is attributed to four key factors: 1) a hybrid architecture that integrates two Conv Blocks and a Transformer Block to synergistically capture both local and global spectral features, thereby mitigating cross-sensitivity and spectral overlap; 2) a high degree of automation, eliminating the need for manual preprocessing and feature selection; 3) exceptional precision, achieving a near-universal R-2 of 0.99 and a sub-1.0x10(-5 )RMSEP for seven alkanes, outperforming CNN, LSTM, and CMT models; 4) effective optimization through Bayesian hyperparameter tuning, which enhances model robustness. This framework presents a highly promising solution for gas-logging applications and is readily extensible to a broader range of infrared-based quantitative spectroscopy tasks.
